get.description:
Upgrades to a WebSocket that streams status updates for the swaps you subscribe to. The envelope is Boltz-compatible:

```json
→ { "op": "subscribe",   "channel": "swap.update", "args": ["01J7Q..."] }
← { "event": "subscribe","channel": "swap.update", "args": ["01J7Q..."],
     "timestamp": "1716124800123" }
← { "event": "update",   "channel": "swap.update",
    "args": [ { "id": "01J7Q...", "status": "invoice.set" } ],
    "timestamp": "1716124800124" }
← { "event": "update",   "channel": "swap.update",
    "args": [ { "id": "01J7Q...", "status": "transaction.confirmed" } ],
    "timestamp": "1716124800456" }
→ { "op": "unsubscribe", "channel": "swap.update", "args": ["01J7Q..."] }
→ { "op": "ping" }
← { "event": "pong" }
```

HealthResponse.description:
`GET /v2/health` / `GET /v2/ready` body — `{"status":"ok"}` or
`{"status":"ready"}`. Liveness/readiness are distinct semantically;
the wire shape is identical.

swapAuth.description:
Per-swap taker credential, 32 bytes hex. Issued **once**, here. Present it as `X-Swap-Auth` on `POST /v2/swap/{id}/rate-decision`;
without it the maker can't tell the taker from anyone else who
happens to know the swap id.
